{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-351064" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨3\\sqrt{t},\\,-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨2,\\,-1⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the components of the the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351064" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3\\sqrt{t},\\,-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\sqrt{t}+{C}_{1},-8\\sqrt{t+1}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨2,-1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\sqrt{0}+{C}_{1},-8\\sqrt{0+1}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},-8+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & ={C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =-8+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2t\\sqrt{t}+2,\\,-8\\sqrt{t+1}+7⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351064" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-351064" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351064" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2t\\sqrt{t}+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351064" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351064" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7-8\\sqrt{t+1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-7196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na=⟨1,2⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨4,3⟩$ when $t=1$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-7196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1,2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t+{C}_{1},2t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨4,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨1+{C}_{1},2(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,3⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},2+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =1+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =2+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv=⟨t+3,2t+1⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-7196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-7196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-7196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v=⟨2t+4,4t+2⟩"] ["math" "v=⟨3t+1,4t+2⟩"] ["math" "v=⟨4t+3,3t+1⟩"] ["math" "v=⟨t+3,2t+1⟩"] ["math" "v=⟨t+4,2t+3⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-7196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v=⟨t+3,2t+1⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},4⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡10,3⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},4⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+{C}_{1},4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡10,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1)+{C}_{1},4(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡10,3⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+{C}_{1},4+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡10 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{2}) \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =4+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5,4t-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡[5(t+1)],4t-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),4t-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-122056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2},2t+1⟩"] ["math" "v=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2},4t-1⟩"] ["math" "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+8),t+2⟩"] ["math" "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),2t+1⟩"] ["math" "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),4t-1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),4t-1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-51343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨{e}^{2t},{e}^{3t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨{e}^{2},{e}^{3}⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-51343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨{e}^{2t},{e}^{3t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨{e}^{2},{e}^{3}⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(1)}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3(1)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{2},{e}^{3}⟩ & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}⟩\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-51343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-51343" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-51343" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}⟩"] ["math" "v=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2},\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}⟩"] ["math" "v=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}⟩"] ["math" "v=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}⟩"] ["math" "v=⟨\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-51343" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨3{sec}^{2}⁡(t),\\,-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨2,\\,1⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the components of the the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3{sec}^{2}⁡(t),\\,-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3{sec}^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int -2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)+{C}_{1},-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨2,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)+{C}_{1},-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},-2+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & ={C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-2+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)+2,\\,-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+3⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-351063" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351063" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\tan(t)+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351063" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351063" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2\\sec(t)+3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-31628" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨8{t}^{3},-1⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,\\,3⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-31628" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨8{t}^{3},-1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 8{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int -1\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨-1,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨2(1)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,3⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},\\,-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-3$ and ${C}_{2}=4.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨2{t}^{4}-3,\\,-t+4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨2(3)^{4}-3,\\,-(3)+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨159,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-31628" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-31628" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-31628" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(3)=⟨-3,\\,4⟩"] ["math" "v(3)=⟨159,\\,1⟩"] ["math" "v(3)=⟨162,\\,-3⟩"] ["math" "v(3)=⟨162,\\,1⟩"] ["math" "v(3)=⟨6,\\,-3⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-31628" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(3)=⟨159,\\,1⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1,1-t⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨0,1⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨{t}^{2}-1,1-t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int ({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (1-t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t+{C}_{1},t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(3)=⟨0,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}-3+{C}_{1},3-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & =⟨6+{C}_{1},-\\frac{3}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-6$ and ${C}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t-6,t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-0-6,0-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,\\frac{5}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-122093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(0)=⟨-6,\\frac{1}{2}⟩"] ["math" "v(0)=⟨-6,\\frac{5}{2}⟩"] ["math" "v(0)=⟨1,-2⟩"] ["math" "v(0)=⟨4,-2⟩"] ["math" "v(0)=⟨4,\\frac{5}{2}⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(0)=⟨-6,\\frac{5}{2}⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-83497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨12(1+2t)^{3},9(1-3t)^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨1,6⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-83497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨12(1+2t)^{3},9(1-3t)^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 12(1+2t)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 9(1-3t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2t)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1-3t)^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨1,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2(0))^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1-3(0))^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,6⟩ & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}+{C}_{1},\\,-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${C}_{2}=7.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2t)^{4}-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-(1-3t)^{3}+7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2(1))^{4}-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-(1-3(1))^{3}+7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨121,15⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-83497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-83497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-83497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(1)=⟨-66,21⟩"] ["math" "v(1)=⟨112,10⟩"] ["math" "v(1)=⟨121,15⟩"] ["math" "v(1)=⟨32,-49⟩"] ["math" "v(1)=⟨85,-8⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-83497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(1)=⟨121,15⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}},t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨1,-1⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}},t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}+{C}_{1},\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨1,-1⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1}}+{C}_{1},\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-1⟩ & =⟨-2+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=3$ and ${C}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}+3,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(4) & =⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4}}+3,\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{13}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-122095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(4)=⟨1,\\frac{7}{2}⟩"] ["math" "v(4)=⟨2,\\frac{13}{2}⟩"] ["math" "v(4)=⟨2,\\frac{9}{2}⟩"] ["math" "v(4)=⟨4,\\frac{13}{2}⟩"] ["math" "v(4)=⟨4,\\frac{15}{2}⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(4)=⟨2,\\frac{13}{2}⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨1-{e}^{t/2},3⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨0,1⟩$ when $t=2,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1-{e}^{t/2},3⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (1-{e}^{t/2})\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 3\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t-2{e}^{t/2}+{C}_{1},3t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨0,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨2-2{e}^{2/2}+{C}_{1},3(2)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & =⟨2-2e+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=2e-2$ and ${C}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨t-2{e}^{t/2}+2e-2,3t-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨0-2{e}^{0/2}+2e-2,3(0)-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2e-4,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-122094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(0)=⟨2e-4,-5⟩"] ["math" "v(0)=⟨2e-4,1⟩"] ["math" "v(0)=⟨e-1,-5⟩"] ["math" "v(0)=⟨e-4\\sqrt{e},-3⟩"] ["math" "v(0)=⟨e-4\\sqrt{e},1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(0)=⟨2e-4,-5⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨2t-6,\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt[3]{t-2}⟩,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,1⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t-6,\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt[3]{t-2}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (2t-6)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt[3]{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-6t+{C}_{1},\\sqrt[3]{(t-2)^{4}}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(3)=⟨-1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨{3}^{2}-6(3)+{C}_{1},\\sqrt[3]{(3-2)^{4}}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,1⟩ & =⟨-9+{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=8$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}-6t+8,\\sqrt[3]{(t-2)^{4}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{2}-6t+8,\\sqrt[3]{(t-2)^{4}}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-6t+8=(t-2)(t-4) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=2,4, \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(t-2)^{4}} & =0\\,⇒\\,t=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=2,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-122102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=1,2"] ["math" "t=2"] ["math" "t=2,4"] ["math" "t=4"] ["text" "No stationary points"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-31634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na=⟨3{t}^{2}-10t,2t-4⟩,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-18,-8⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-31634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3{t}^{2}-10t,2t-4⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (3{t}^{2}-10t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (2t-4)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}-4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(3)=⟨-18,-8⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨(3)^{3}-5(3)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,(3)^{2}-4(3)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-18,-8⟩ & =⟨-18+{C}_{1},\\,-3+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}-4t-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}-4t-5⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}={t}^{2}(t-5) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0,5, \\\\ {t}^{2}-4t-5=(t+1)(t-5) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-1,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=5,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-31634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-31634" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-31634" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=-1"] ["math" "t=0"] ["math" "t=1"] ["math" "t=5"] ["text" "No stationary points"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-31634" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨-1,2⟩,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,2⟩$ when $t=2,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨-1,2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-t+{C}_{1},2t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨-1,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨-2+{C}_{1},2(2)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨-2+{C}_{1},4+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨1-t,2t-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1-t,2t-2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-t & =0\\,⇒\\,t=1, \\\\ 2t-2=2(t-1) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=1,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-122096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=1"] ["math" "t=2"] ["math" "t=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{3}{2}"] ["text" "No stationary points"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-122096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-83506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩,\\,0\\le t<\\pi .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨1,1⟩$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-83506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=⟨1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=0,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-83506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-83506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-83506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=0"] ["math" "t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["text" "No stationary points"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-83506" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3\\sqrt{t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,\\,-14⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary when $t=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3\\sqrt{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 3\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{1/2}+{C}_{1},\\,2{t}^{3/2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨-1,\\,-14⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨{1}^{1/2}+{C}_{1},\\,2\\cdot {1}^{3/2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,\\,-14⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,2+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=-2$ and ${C}_{2}=-16.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{t}^{1/2}-2,\\,2{t}^{3/2}-16⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{1/2}-2,\\,2{t}^{3/2}-16⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{1/2}-2=0 & \\,⇒\\,{t}^{1/2}=2\\,⇒\\,t=4, \\\\ 2{t}^{3/2}-16=0 & \\,⇒\\,{t}^{3/2}=8\\,⇒\\,t=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=4.$ So, this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28a05b9de0c32eb06f9e7f52dc05275370043c1b79c38581917bafef4137ccd7"} {"question" "q-351102" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "q-351102" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/state.json" "file_sha256" "acdb2b13f0ad8e23afb22cb64dcf5a855b7cd256cdd97f3fc605bf165976cf4b"} {"question" "e-622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 10, 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 10,20\\rangle$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/example-622.json" "file_sha256" "8b279df60207ec2f66b650b779fdde0040d2cd4d3c16392d33785bcc15d4da45"} {"question" "e-622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨10,0⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 10\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 0\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10t+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{v} (1)= \\langle 10,20\\rangle.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨10(1)+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨10,20⟩ & =⟨10+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =10+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nC_2 = 20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨10t,\\,20⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/example-622.json" "file_sha256" "8b279df60207ec2f66b650b779fdde0040d2cd4d3c16392d33785bcc15d4da45"} {"question" "e-623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 2e^{2t}, 3e^{3t}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle e^2, 2e^3 \\rangle$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/example-623.json" "file_sha256" "29509420a4b39401c04b5210c73941ce68e092ab7fb27d7630cdbcafebb4c030"} {"question" "e-623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2{e}^{2t},3{e}^{3t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 3{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2t}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{3t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{v}(1) = \\langle e^2, 2e^3 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨{e}^{2(1)}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{3(1)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{2},2{e}^{3}⟩ & =⟨{e}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=0$ and $C_2 = e^3.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{e}^{2t},\\,{e}^{3t}+{e}^{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨{e}^{2(2)},\\,{e}^{3(2)}+{e}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{4},\\,{e}^{6}+{e}^{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/example-623.json" "file_sha256" "29509420a4b39401c04b5210c73941ce68e092ab7fb27d7630cdbcafebb4c030"} {"question" "e-624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 1, 2t \\rangle, \\quad -\\infty <        t <        \\infty.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 2,0 \\rangle$ when $t=1,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/example-624.json" "file_sha256" "34fcce7755e492a114359f1262ad9c86b3867e31c6cf890742f7dc985a013bad"} {"question" "e-624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1,2t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{v} (1)= \\langle 2,0\\rangle.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,{1}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,0⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=1$ and $C_2=-1.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨t+1,\\,{t}^{2}-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨t+1,\\,{t}^{2}-1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t+1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-1, \\\\ {t}^{2}-1=(t+1)(t-1) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t= -1,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064400/example-624.json" "file_sha256" "34fcce7755e492a114359f1262ad9c86b3867e31c6cf890742f7dc985a013bad"}] "usage" {"e-624" [] "e-622" [] "q-51343" [] "e-623" [] "q-31628" []} "basis" 2103 "retractions" [[17592186088146 :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$a(t) = ⟨e^{2t}, e^{3t}⟩$.\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v = ⟨e^{2}, e^{3}⟩$ when $t = 1$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t$."] [17592186088072 :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$a(t) = ⟨8t^{3},-1⟩$.\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v = ⟨ - 1, 3⟩$ when $t = 1$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t = 3$."] [17592186087525 :question/problem "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 10, 0\\rangle$.\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 10,20\\rangle$ when $t=1$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t$."] [17592186087525 :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨10, 0⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫10dt, ∫0dt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨10t + C_{1}, C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} (1)= \\langle 10,20\\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(1) &= ⟨10(1) + C_{1}, C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨10, 20⟩ &= ⟨10 + C_{1}, C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 &= 10 + C_{1} \\\\\nC_{1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n$C_2 = 20$.\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ⟨10t, 20⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087527 :question/problem "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 2e^{2t}, 3e^{3t}\\rangle$.\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle e^2, 2e^3 \\rangle$ when $t=1$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=2$."] [17592186087527 :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨2e^{2t}, 3e^{3t}⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫2e^{2t}dt, ∫3e^{3t}dt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨e^{2t} + C_{1}, e^{3t} + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(1) = \\langle e^2, 2e^3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(1) &= ⟨e^{2(1)} + C_{1}, e^{3(1)} + C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨e^{2}, 2e^{3}⟩ &= ⟨e^{2} + C_{1}, e^{3} + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=0$ and $C_2 = e^3$. Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ⟨e^{2t}, e^{3t} + e^{3}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(2) &= ⟨e^{2(2)}, e^{3(2)} + e^{3}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨e^{4}, e^{6} + e^{3}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087528 :question/problem "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 1, 2t \\rangle, \\quad -\\infty < t < \\infty$.\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 2,0 \\rangle$ when $t=1$, calculate the times at which the particle is stationary."] [17592186087528 :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨1, 2t⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫1dt, ∫2tdt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t + C_{1}, t^{2} + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} (1)= \\langle 2,0\\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(1) &= ⟨1 + C_{1}, 1^{2} + C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨2, 0⟩ &= ⟨1 + C_{1}, 1 + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=1$ and $C_2=-1$. Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ⟨t + 1, t^{2} - 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= 0 \\\\\n⟨t + 1, t^{2} - 1⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt + 1 &= 0⇒t =-1, \\\\\nt^{2} - 1 = (t + 1)(t - 1) &= 0⇒t = ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t= -1$, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186085338 17592186085339 17592186085340 17592186085341 17592186085342 17592186085808 17592186085809 17592186085810 17592186085811 17592186085812] "immutable_fields" [[17592186085337 :answer-field/correct] [17592186085337 :answer-field/id] [17592186085337 :answer-field/key] [17592186085337 :answer-field/type] [17592186085807 :answer-field/correct] [17592186085807 :answer-field/id] [17592186085807 :answer-field/key] [17592186085807 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
